d Lane","h":8,"osm":"way/1161594472","kind":"semidetached_house","council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16016,52.9253]},"63733":{"where":"Ruddington Lane","h":10,"osm":"way/80847497","council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16058,52.92505]},"63734":{"y":1781,"f":"exact","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"1781","h":10,"osm":"way/80847924","kind":"office","listEntry":1247201,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1247201","qid":"Q26539530","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wilford_House%2C_Clifton_Lane%2C_Nottingham_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3412839.jpg","credit":{"file":"Wilford House, Clifton Lane, Nottingham - geograph.org.uk - 3412839.jpg","year":2003,"author":"Stephen Richards","licence":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},"stories":[{"layer":"wilford","text":"The house was designed in 1781 for the Smith family as Wilford Hall, the centre of an estate with stables, lodges for the house staff and a private chapel.[^walk] It is a red-brick country house of three storeys and five bays, listed Grade II, and its stable block of the same year is listed too. The Smiths' bank in Nottingham is believed to have been the first bank outside London, and Samuel Smith, an MP for 44 years, is believed to have built the house; by 1790 it was his.[^throsby] The banker Abel Smith died at Wilford on 12 July 1788.[^s1][^s2] His wife's younger sister, Elizabeth Bird, was the mother of William Wilberforce, the slavery abolitionist, who stayed at the house for several months between 1786 and 1789.[^s1][^s2][^s3] In 1853 it was the seat of Henry Smith, a large, handsome brick mansion; he owned about 70 acres of the parish, and in 1828 he built an infant school in the village.[^white]\n\nThomas Forman, who founded the Nottingham Post, later bought the house from the Smiths, and it passed to the Forman Hardy family; at some point it was renamed Wilford House.[^s1] It became offices in the 1980s, the Wilford Place estate was built on its grounds in 2006,[^trail] and in 2021 the house was given a multi-million pound refurbishment.[^s1]","source":{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford","kind":"Wikipedia"},"sources":[{"title":"Abel Smith (1717–1788)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel_Smith_(1717%E2%80%931788)","kind":"Wikipedia"},{"title":"Smith's Bank","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smith%27s_Bank","kind":"Wikipedia"},{"title":"Listed buildings in Nottingham (Clifton North ward)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Listed_buildings_in_Nottingham_(Clifton_North_ward)","kind":"Wikipedia"}],"cites":{"walk":{"title":"Wilford Appendix (transportnottingham.com)","url":"https://www.transportnottingham.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Wilford-Further-Info.pdf","kind":"transportnottingham.com","read":"2026-11-16"},"throsby":{"title":"Thoroton's History of Nottinghamshire, vol. 1, Parishes: Clifton (Throsby, 1790)","url":"https://www.british-history.ac.uk/node/66285","kind":"british-history.ac.uk","read":"2026-11-16"},"white":{"title":"White's Directory of Nottinghamshire, 1853: Wilford (GENUKI)","url":"https://www.genuki.org.uk/big/eng/NTT/Wilford/White1853","kind":"genuki.org.uk","read":"2026-11-16"},"trail":{"title":"Wilford Heritage Trail: Crossroads (This Is Wilford)","url":"https://www.thisiswilford.org.uk/crossroads","kind":"thisiswilford.org.uk","read":"2026-11-16"},"s1":{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilford","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s2":{"title":"Abel Smith (1717–1788)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel_Smith_(1717%E2%80%931788)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"s3":{"title":"Smith's Bank","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smith%27s_Bank","kind":"Wikipedia"}},"when":"1781","whenSrc":{"title":"Listed buildings in Nottingham (Clifton North ward)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Listed_buildings_in_Nottingham_(Clifton_North_ward)","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Wilford House"}],"sum":"Wilford House is an office building on Clifton Lane. The building was built in 1781, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16092,52.92524]},"63735":{"where":"Clifton Lane","h":4.2,"levels":"1","osm":"way/1162862746","kind":"house","council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16243,52.92529]},"63736":{"where":"Clifton Lane","h":4.2,"levels":"1","osm":"way/1162862748","kind":"house","council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16251,52.92526]},"63737":{"where":"Clifton Lane","h":4.2,"levels":"1","osm":"way/80849598","kind":"house","council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16259,52.92524]},"63738":{"where":"Deane Road","h":10,"osm":"way/108690415","council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16258,52.92513]},"63739":{"where":"Clifton Lane","h":4.2,"levels":"1","osm":"way/1162862749","kind":"house","council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16234,52.92532]},"63740":{"where":"Clifton Lane","h":4.2,"levels":"1","osm":"way/1162862750","kind":"house","council":"Nottingham City Council","coll":[{"title":"Wilford","url":"https://picturenottingham.co.uk/image-themes/wilford.html"}],"xy":[-1.16223,52.92536]},"63741":{"where